  "slug": "allosteric-molecule-stabilizers-a5cb",
  "summary": "Long-read sequencing methods now expose sequence-dependent error and single-molecule structural heterogeneity in RNA and viral vector genomes.",
  "synthesis": "The common thread is that sequence context shapes what sequencing measurements report, and computational models are used to separate that bias from biology. In direct RNA nanopore reads, non-random error hotspots are linked to RNA folding after translocation, which can bias modification detection on CY1 viral RNA. In nascent RNA-seq, a probabilistic mixture model identifies two conserved Pol II disassociation region types, T-rich and GC-rich, that respond differently to stress, and base composition is reported to influence the position and precision of disassociation. A long-read PacBio pipeline extends this theme to viral vector genomes, tiling reads and applying a formal grammar to classify single AAV vector genomes into structural classes. What is new here is the explicit attribution of systematic nanopore errors to post-translocation folding, together with grammar-based single-molecule structural classification of AAV genomes.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2025-12-09"
}
